Choose the entity, basis and information in a chemical quantity (@chemistry-quantity-bridge: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../general-chemistry/quantities/Mass-amount-and-entities.lingua#r_5FWGQX84V6GZGC1RABD34ZKE04") { r_0FA652331F0EBF3613EA1DA8B2
Before converting a specimen's mass into an amount, name what is being counted. A formula unit, an atom, a molecule and an ion are different entities. The amount of a compound is not automatically the same as the amount of each element or the total amount of all dissolved ions. Chemical notation and the chosen physical model determine the relation.

For one mole of formula units represented by CaCl₂, the composition contains one mole of calcium atoms and two moles of chlorine atoms. If the specified aqueous model treats the solute as fully separated Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, the corresponding amounts are one mole and two moles of those ions. Their signed charge account is 2 × 1 − 1 × 2 = 0 in units of mole of elementary charges. This is bookkeeping within the stated model, not a claim that all real solutions behave ideally or contain intact isolated CaCl₂ molecules.

A mass fraction is a component's mass divided by the total mass on a stated basis. An amount fraction uses amounts of specified entities instead. A percentage becomes useful only after its numerator, denominator and chemical meaning are known. For a hypothetical mixture containing 2 mol of molecular species X with molar mass 10 g/mol and 1 mol of Y with molar mass 40 g/mol, X has amount fraction 2/3 but mass fraction 20/60 = 1/3. X and Y here are invented labels and teaching molar masses, not identified substances.

Use the existing [Units and dimensions lesson](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1) before mass-to-amount conversions. The earlier mathematical foundation supplies fractions, proportions and equations. In scientific notation, 10³ means one thousand and 10⁻³ means one thousandth; moving between grams and kilograms must change the compatible molar-mass unit as well. The SI-defined Avogadro constant is exact, while the mass, composition and molar mass used for a specimen generally carry uncertainty.

For a bounded teaching example, suppose the specified mass lies between 9.9 and 10.1 g and the positive molar mass between 19.9 and 20.1 g/mol. The possible amount is bounded by 9.9/20.1 and 10.1/19.9 mol when these combinations are allowed. Dividing the two lower endpoints and the two upper endpoints would miss the extreme ratios. These are hard input bounds, not a probability interval or a pair of standard uncertainties.

**Work:** reconstruct the entity and fraction accounts without copying the numerical answers. Change the quantity basis and check what remains invariant. Work the hard-bound part of the shared precision lesson now; return to its standard-uncertainty and correlation exercises after the corresponding metrology preparation. Before the shared conservation/extent lessons, use element counts and ordinary equations; their general matrix and multiple-reaction treatments remain later continuations.

**Read:** [Chemistry 2e](../general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94) section 3.1 and its composition exercises, alongside the existing amount, isotope and unit Records. Keep the book's assigned reading separate from a claim that all of its problems have been solved.

**Shared preparation:** [Mathematical quantities, arithmetic and reasoning](../../mathematics/core/Numbers-models-and-mathematical-arguments.lingua#r_0BC762DF1B7E019C36477207F3); [Chemical amounts](../general-chemistry/quantities/Mass-amount-and-entities.lingua#r_5FWGQX84V6GZGC1RABD34ZKE04); [Precision and chemical claims](../general-chemistry/quantities/Precision-uncertainty-and-chemical-claims.lingua#r_25GXFBHM1573BAYA8PFWH0YW97).

